import "core/list.ct"
import "core/render.ct"


# # The list library's displays of its own calls
  A display of a call says what a function did, where a display of a value says what the function left
  behind. @core/list.ct@'s combinators are the case for it: each of them returns a list, and a list is
  the one thing about the call a reader could already see. What they cannot see is the correspondence —
  which element became which, which was kept, what each step added to the accumulator — and that is the
  whole of what the combinator does.
  Each is declared by an arm of [/Catln/Doc/dshowCall] whose argument is a call of the function. There is
  no annotation marking the function and no name derived from it: the arm is the declaration, as an arm
  of [/Catln/Doc/dshow] is for a type. The arms here are bounded to [/Data/List/ConsList] so they are the
  list's displays and not every container's — a [/Data/Maybe] mapped over looks nothing like a table of
  rows and would say so in a page of its own.
# # Why this is a page of its own
  The arms name [/Catln/Doc/dshowCall], so this page reaches @core/webdoc.ct@, and @core/list.ct@ is
  reached by nearly every page there is. Writing them there would put the doc server's vocabulary
  underneath the list library. So this sits where @core/render.ct@ and @core/result.ct@ sit and by the
  same arrangement: it reaches @core/list.ct@ freely, and nothing in the library reaches it.
module(Data/List)
  # [fmap] over a [ConsList] shown as the table of what each element became: a row per element, the
    element on the left and the result of applying [fn] to it on the right.
    This is the display of the call and not of the list it returns. The recursion that @core/list.ct@'s
    instance is written as is the generic display — a call per element, each holding a shorter list than
    the one above it — and it is a display of how the definition is written rather than of what it does.
    The table is the second, and is what a reader opening a mapping wants.
    Bounded to elements that are [/Data/ToString], which is what a row can hold (see
    [/Catln/Doc/Show/Step]). A mapping over elements that render no text does not match this arm and is
    shown the way it was before there was one.
  # A mapping over a [ConsList] of things that render text is a call with a display, which is what
    joining [/Catln/Doc/DShowCall] says. The bound on the elements is the whole of which mappings this
    is claimed for: a mapping over elements that render no text is not a member and keeps the display
    every other call gets.
  every(/Data/Functor/fmap(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v)), isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShowCall])

  /Catln/Doc/dshowCall(s= /Data/Functor/fmap(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v)))= /Catln/Doc/Show/Steps(steps= fmapSteps(lst= lst, fn(v)= fn(v= v), index= 0))

  # The rows of that table, walking the list and applying [fn] to each element as it goes.
    It applies [fn] rather than being handed what [fn] already returned. An arm of
    [/Catln/Doc/dshowCall] is given the call's arguments and nothing else, so working the outputs out is
    the show's own job — which is also what lets the arm above say what a mapping does without the
    debugger arranging anything for it.
    [index] is threaded rather than derived, since a [ConsList] knows nothing about where in a list it
    sits, and the rows are wanted numbered from the front.
  fmapSteps(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v -> $_A) -> $_B, index -> Integer) -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/Step..]
  fmapSteps(lst= Nil, fn(v), index)= Nil
  fmapSteps(lst= Cons(head, tail), fn(v), index)= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/Step(index= index, input= head.toString, output= fn(v= head).toString), tail= fmapSteps(lst= tail, fn(v)= fn(v= v), index= index + 1))

  # [/Data/Monad/mmap] over a [ConsList] shown as the same table as a mapping: a row per element, the
    element on the left and the list [fn] made of it on the right.
    The result of the call is those lists joined end to end, and joined they no longer say which
    element contributed what — or, where an element contributed the empty list, that it was there at
    all. The table keeps each element beside its own contribution.
    This is also how a [filter] is shown. [filter] is a single call of [mmap] whose function makes a
    one-element list of what it keeps and the empty list of what it drops, so the call of [mmap]
    inside it reads as the filter's verdicts: a kept element beside a list holding it, a dropped one
    beside the empty list. The filter is not given a display of its own, since it would be this one
    again with nothing added.
    Bounded as the mapping is, to elements that are [/Data/ToString], and to [ConsList] so a
    [/Data/Maybe] bound over keeps the display of any other call.
  # A binding over a [ConsList] of things that render text is a call with a display.
  every(/Data/Monad/mmap(m -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v)), isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShowCall])

  /Catln/Doc/dshowCall(s= /Data/Monad/mmap(m -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(v)))= /Catln/Doc/Show/Steps(steps= mmapSteps(m= m, fn(v)= fn(v= v), index= 0))

  # The rows of that table, applying [fn] to each element as it goes, as [fmapSteps] does.
  mmapSteps[$A -> /Data/ToString](m -> ConsList[$T -> $_A], fn(v -> $_A) -> $_B, index -> Integer) -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/Step..]
  mmapSteps(m= Nil, fn(v), index)= Nil
  mmapSteps(m= Cons(head, tail), fn(v), index)= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/Step(index= index, input= head.toString, output= fn(v= head).toString), tail= mmapSteps(m= tail, fn(v)= fn(v= v), index= index + 1))

  # [foldl] over a [ConsList] shown as the steps it took: a row per element, each with the
    accumulator it was handed, the element it combined in, and the accumulator it handed on to the
    next row. The table starts at [init] and ends at the fold's result.
    The fold returns only the last of those accumulators, and every one before it is the whole of
    what the fold did. Bounded as the mapping is to elements that are [/Data/ToString], and to an
    [init] that is one as well, which is what an accumulator's cell can hold.
  # A left fold over a [ConsList] of things that render text is a call with a display.
  every(/Data/List/foldl(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(acc, v), init -> /Data/ToString), isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShowCall])

  /Catln/Doc/dshowCall(s= /Data/List/foldl(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(acc, v), init -> /Data/ToString))= foldlShow(steps= foldlSteps(lst= lst, fn(acc, v)= fn(acc= acc, v= v), acc= init, index= 0), init= init.toString)

  # The display around a left fold's rows. Its result is the last row's output, read off the rows
    rather than worked out by folding again: the rows already hold every accumulator the fold made.
    A fold over nothing hands [init] straight on.
  foldlShow(steps -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..], init -> String) -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldSteps..
  foldlShow(steps, init)= /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldSteps(init= init, steps= steps, result= lastOutput(steps= steps, prev= init), fromRight= /Data/Primitive/False)

  # The output of the last row, or [prev] where there are none.
  lastOutput(steps -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..], prev -> String) -> String
  lastOutput(steps= Nil, prev)= prev
  lastOutput(steps= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep(index, acc, input, output), tail), prev)= lastOutput(steps= tail, prev= output)

  # The rows of a left fold, carrying the accumulator forward as [foldl] does.
  foldlSteps(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(acc -> $_B, v -> $_A) -> $_B, acc -> $_B, index -> Integer) -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..]
  foldlSteps(lst= Nil, fn(acc, v), acc, index)= Nil
  foldlSteps(lst= Cons(head, tail), fn(acc, v), acc, index)= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep(index= index, acc= acc.toString, input= head.toString, output= fn(acc= acc, v= head).toString), tail= foldlSteps(lst= tail, fn(acc, v)= fn(acc= acc, v= v), acc= fn(acc= acc, v= head), index= index + 1))

  # [foldr] over a [ConsList] shown the same way, with the accumulator running the other way: the
    last element is combined into [init] first, and each row hands its output to the row above it.
    The rows are still one per element in element order, which is the order the fold's calls are
    nested in and so the order a debugger finds them in.
  # A right fold over a [ConsList] of things that render text is a call with a display.
  every(/Data/List/foldr(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(acc, v), init -> /Data/ToString), isa= [/Catln/Doc/DShowCall])

  /Catln/Doc/dshowCall(s= /Data/List/foldr(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(acc, v), init -> /Data/ToString))= foldrShow(steps= foldrSteps(lst= lst, fn(acc, v)= fn(acc= acc, v= v), init= init, index= 0), init= init.toString)

  # The display around a right fold's rows, whose result is the FIRST row's output: the head is the
    element combined last.
    Read off the rows for the reason [foldlShow] gives, and for one more. The arm cannot call
    [foldr] for it: built from the arm, where the step is a function of unknown result, [foldr]'s
    [Cons] arm returns what the step returns and so is not known to render as text. Only its [Nil]
    arm is, and the call then fails on any list that is not empty.
  foldrShow(steps -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..], init -> String) -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldSteps..
  foldrShow(steps, init)= /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldSteps(init= init, steps= steps, result= firstOutput(steps= steps, init= init), fromRight= /Data/Primitive/True)

  # The output of the first row, or [init] where there are none.
  firstOutput(steps -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..], init -> String) -> String
  firstOutput(steps= Nil, init)= init
  firstOutput(steps= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep(index, acc, input, output), tail), init)= output

  # The rows of a right fold, worked out the way the fold runs: from the last element back to the
    first, carrying the accumulator as [foldlSteps] does. The list is first turned around onto
    [stack], and each row is then consed on in front of the rows already made, so they come out in
    element order with the fold's order still in the accumulators. [index] counts down from the
    last element's.
  foldrSteps(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(acc -> $_B, v -> $_A) -> $_B, init -> $_B, index -> Integer) -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..]
  foldrSteps(lst, fn(acc, v), init, index)= foldrWalk(stack= onto(lst= lst, stack= Nil), fn(acc, v)= fn(acc= acc, v= v), acc= init, index= index + lst.length - 1, rows= Nil)

  # [lst] turned around onto [stack], so the last element is on top.
  onto(lst -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], stack -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString]) -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString]
  onto(lst= Nil, stack)= stack
  onto(lst= Cons(head, tail), stack)= onto(lst= tail, stack= Cons(head= head, tail= stack))

  # The walk over that stack: each element combined into the accumulator the elements after it made.
  foldrWalk(stack -> ConsList[$T -> /Data/ToString], fn(acc -> $_B, v -> $_A) -> $_B, acc -> $_B, index -> Integer, rows -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..]) -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..]
  foldrWalk(stack= Nil, fn(acc, v), acc, index, rows)= rows
  foldrWalk(stack= Cons(head, tail), fn(acc, v), acc, index, rows)= foldrWalk(stack= tail, fn(acc, v)= fn(acc= acc, v= v), acc= fn(acc= acc, v= head), index= index - 1, rows= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep(index= index, acc= acc.toString, input= head.toString, output= fn(acc= acc, v= head).toString), tail= rows))

  # The rows written out as text, which is how this page checks the ones it builds.
    It is not an arm of [/Data/toString] and [/Catln/Doc/Show/Steps] is not a [/Data/ToString]: a value
    that renders text is shown as that text, so making these rows one would replace the display they
    exist to be. So this is a rendering the page keeps to itself.
    A test and not a golden, because the arm above is reached only from a debugger and so cannot be
    written into a [#print] here. What a [#print] could reach is [fmapSteps], and what it builds is
    everything the arm does beyond handing it over — so that is what is asserted.
  showSteps(steps -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/Step..]) -> String
  showSteps(steps= Nil)= ""
  showSteps(steps= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/Step(index, input, output), tail))= index.toString ++ ":" ++ input ++ "->" ++ output ++ ";" ++ showSteps(steps= tail)

  exampleFmapSteps =
    #example
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= fmapSteps(lst= [1, 2, 3], fn(v)= v + 1, index= 0)) == "0:1->2;1:2->3;2:3->4;")
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= fmapSteps(lst= [], fn(v)= v + 1, index= 0)) == "")
    0

  exampleMmapSteps =
    #example
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= mmapSteps(m= [1, 2], fn(v)= [v, v], index= 0)) == "0:1->[1, 1];1:2->[2, 2];")
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= mmapSteps(m= [1, 2, 3], fn(v)= [], index= 0)) == "0:1->[];1:2->[];2:3->[];")
    #assert(test= showSteps(steps= mmapSteps(m= [], fn(v)= [v], index= 0)) == "")
    0

  # The rows of a fold written out as text, as [showSteps] writes a mapping's.
  showFoldSteps(steps -> /Data/List[$T -> /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep..]) -> String
  showFoldSteps(steps= Nil)= ""
  showFoldSteps(steps= Cons(head= /Catln/Doc/Show/FoldStep(index, acc, input, output), tail))= index.toString ++ ":" ++ acc ++ "+" ++ input ++ "=" ++ output ++ ";" ++ showFoldSteps(steps= tail)

  # A sum reads the same from either end, but the rows do not: a left fold's first row is handed
    [init], and a right fold's last row is.
  exampleFoldSums =
    #example
    #assert(test= showFoldSteps(steps= foldlSteps(lst= [1, 2, 3], fn(acc, v)= acc + v, acc= 0, index= 0)) == "0:0+1=1;1:1+2=3;2:3+3=6;")
    #assert(test= showFoldSteps(steps= foldrSteps(lst= [1, 2, 3], fn(acc, v)= acc + v, init= 0, index= 0)) == "0:5+1=6;1:3+2=5;2:0+3=3;")
    #assert(test= showFoldSteps(steps= foldlSteps(lst= [], fn(acc, v)= acc + v, acc= 0, index= 0)) == "")
    #assert(test= showFoldSteps(steps= foldrSteps(lst= [], fn(acc, v)= acc + v, init= 0, index= 0)) == "")
    0

  # The result a display gives is read off its rows: the last row's for a left fold, the first row's
    for a right one, and [init] for a fold over nothing.
  exampleFoldResults =
    #example
    #assert(test= lastOutput(steps= foldlSteps(lst= ["a", "b", "c"], fn(acc, v)= acc ++ v, acc= "", index= 0), prev= "") == "abc")
    #assert(test= firstOutput(steps= foldrSteps(lst= ["a", "b", "c"], fn(acc, v)= acc ++ v, init= "", index= 0), init= "") == "cba")
    #assert(test= lastOutput(steps= foldlSteps(lst= [], fn(acc, v)= acc ++ v, acc= "", index= 0), prev= "s") == "s")
    #assert(test= firstOutput(steps= foldrSteps(lst= [], fn(acc, v)= acc ++ v, init= "", index= 0), init= "s") == "s")
    0

  # Joining text is where the two ends differ in what they make, not only in how.
  exampleFoldJoins =
    #example
    #assert(test= showFoldSteps(steps= foldlSteps(lst= ["a", "b", "c"], fn(acc, v)= acc ++ v, acc= "", index= 0)) == "0:+a=a;1:a+b=ab;2:ab+c=abc;")
    #assert(test= showFoldSteps(steps= foldrSteps(lst= ["a", "b", "c"], fn(acc, v)= acc ++ v, init= "", index= 0)) == "0:cb+a=cba;1:c+b=cb;2:+c=c;")
    0
